  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Electric field due to given potentials is&nbsp;</p><p><math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo>-</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mfenced close=\"|\" open=\"|\" separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>&nbsp;for&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8739;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi><mo>&#8739;</mo><mo>&#8804;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>. Here&nbsp;&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math> is proportional to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>z</mi></math>, so&nbsp;charge distribution is continuous for&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"|\" open=\"|\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mfenced><mo>&#8804;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math><br /><br /><math><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></math>&nbsp;for&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"|\" open=\"|\" separators=\"|\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mfenced><mo>&#8805;</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>. Here,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>E</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> is constant.&nbsp;</p><p>So, using the Poisson&rsquo;s equation&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mo>&#8711;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mi>&#961;</mi><msub><mi>&#949;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac></math>, we get&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#961;</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>.<br /><br /><math><mo>&#8756;</mo></math> The source is an infinity large non-conducting thick plate.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p>Hence, consider a cylindrical Gaussian surface:</p><p>Applying Gauss&#39;s law for&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8739;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi><mo>&#8739;</mo><mo>&#8804;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#961;</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#949;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mfenced><mfenced separators=\"|\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#961;</mi><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>Az</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#949;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfrac></math><br /><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#961;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#949;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math> for <math><mfenced close=\"|\" open=\"|\" separators=\"|\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">z</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>&#8804;</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi></math>.</p>"
  }
